00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
If we want to represent strings, there are two ways that we can do that Python accepts both single quotes and double quotes.

00:08.000 --> 00:26.000
If you are defining a string, make sure that whatever type of quote that you use, make sure that it matches, or you're going to get an error.

00:26.000 --> 00:39.000
I prefer double quotes because if you have a sentence like this, is this going to work when I enter this into my repel?

00:39.000 --> 00:44.000
No, it's going to blow up because Python thinks that the string has ended here.

00:44.000 --> 00:55.000
So if you're using single quotes and you have a single quote inside of your string, you're going to want to escape it with the backward slash.

00:56.000 --> 01:10.000
And now Python does the right thing, or you can just use double quotes, and then you don't have that problem, because single quotes can be nested inside of double quotes without issue and without escaping.

01:10.000 --> 01:16.000
Because Python knows that the match for this first double quote is this second double quote.

01:17.000 --> 01:40.000
If you have long strings, you can start them with three quotes either double or single, and then you can press enter and then to end your very long string, you want to use three quotes again.

01:40.000 --> 01:47.000
If we take a look at the string, we'll see that it doesn't look like this when we print it out.

01:47.000 --> 01:58.000
It's separated by a backslash n, backslash n is the symbol for new line, backslash t is the symbol for tab.

01:58.000 --> 02:09.000
If we print this out, we'll see that it looks how we expected to, but that's not how Python stores it under the hood.

02:11.000 --> 02:18.000
So printing it out is basically asking for how it might look like when it's rendered.

02:18.000 --> 02:22.000
Now print is something that you'll be using a lot.

02:22.000 --> 02:39.000
You can print out strings, you can print out variables, and something that I find very handy in print is that you can actually pass in multiple values to print.

02:39.000 --> 02:55.000
And if they're separated by a comma, the output of print will put a space in between them, so I can say print high and then A and it will print high space five.

02:55.000 --> 03:02.000
This is just kind of a quick and handy shortcut when you don't want to use an F string, when you don't want to format something.

03:02.000 --> 03:11.000
Just want to print it out, see what it looks like. I use this all the time, but for some reason, it is not common syntax.

03:11.000 --> 03:19.000
